1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
 - Hello and welcome to chapter seven.

2
00:00:02,240 --> 00:00:04,900
 This is the chapter where it all really starts to pay off.

3
00:00:04,900 --> 00:00:07,980
 We have been learning bits and pieces

4
00:00:07,980 --> 00:00:12,780
 and doing little two lines, three lines, four lines of code

5
00:00:12,780 --> 00:00:15,360
 to learn the basic building blocks of Python

6
00:00:15,360 --> 00:00:18,620
 and learn some of the syntax and find lots of terms.

7
00:00:18,620 --> 00:00:22,120
 But now we're actually going to start doing something.

8
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:25,040
 So if you look at what we've been doing so far,

9
00:00:25,040 --> 00:00:28,520
 you know, we are inside this little computer

10
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:31,620
 and you type up, you know, the Python says what next

11
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:33,560
 and you give it its command and it does something

12
00:00:33,560 --> 00:00:35,880
 and you do something else and does something

13
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:37,440
 and you do this three or four times

14
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:39,240
 unless you write a loop and then it goes like,

15
00:00:39,240 --> 00:00:41,480
 you know, 10, 20 times and that's it.

16
00:00:41,480 --> 00:00:43,600
 And then maybe we write a thing

17
00:00:43,600 --> 00:00:45,320
 that reads something from our keyboard,

18
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:47,320
 gives us something back and then we write something

19
00:00:47,320 --> 00:00:50,280
 and print something out, print a few things out.

20
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:53,200
 And so we've been pretty much using the keyboard,

21
00:00:53,200 --> 00:00:56,440
 the screen, the CPU and the memory.

22
00:00:56,440 --> 00:00:58,700
 That's kind of where we've been living.

23
00:00:58,700 --> 00:01:00,600
 And while it's important to talk to the keyboard

24
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:04,540
 and the screen, the real world is things like databases

25
00:01:04,540 --> 00:01:07,360
 that live out here, files that live on our systems

26
00:01:07,360 --> 00:01:09,560
 and, you know, connecting to the network

27
00:01:09,560 --> 00:01:12,320
 and reading data from the network.

28
00:01:12,320 --> 00:01:14,400
 And so that's what we're starting to do right now

29
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:18,120
 is we're starting to be able to work outside

30
00:01:18,120 --> 00:01:20,820
 kind of our code and create things that are permanent.

31
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:23,160
 And so we're going to be talking,

32
00:01:23,160 --> 00:01:25,260
 initially we're going to work on files.

33
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:27,440
 We'll later talk to databases and the network

34
00:01:27,440 --> 00:01:31,000
 and other stuff, but for now we are talking about files.

35
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,760
 And so really kind of, we're stepping out a little bit

36
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:36,300
 and creating, reading things that are permanent

37
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:38,980
 and creating things that are permanent.

38
00:01:38,980 --> 00:01:40,680
 The kinds of files that we're going to talk about

39
00:01:40,680 --> 00:01:42,800
 mostly are text files.

40
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:47,240
 And you can think of these as a sequence of lines in a file

41
00:01:47,240 --> 00:01:49,600
 that are easily read by Python.

42
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:51,880
 You've been making text files all along.

43
00:01:51,880 --> 00:01:56,880
 You're, you know, hello.py, that file's a text file too.

44
00:01:56,880 --> 00:01:59,600
 You're using a text editor to create that file.

45
00:01:59,600 --> 00:02:01,120
 You put your Python commands in a file,

46
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:03,480
 you run those files, and that's what it is.

47
00:02:03,480 --> 00:02:06,920
 And so a file can be thought of as a bunch of lines,

48
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:09,000
 you know, one, two, three, four, five, six, seven,

49
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:10,240
 a blank line here.

50
00:02:10,240 --> 00:02:11,460
 That's possible.

51
00:02:11,460 --> 00:02:16,460
 And, but the reality is that these are actually just lines

52
00:02:16,460 --> 00:02:18,520
 and we have a special character called the new line

53
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:20,260
 that we'll talk about in a second.

54
00:02:21,640 --> 00:02:25,160
 So to read a file, you have to call the open function.

55
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:27,920
 And open returns what we call a file handle.

56
00:02:27,920 --> 00:02:30,280
 Open doesn't actually read the file.

57
00:02:30,280 --> 00:02:34,500
 Open makes it possible so that you can read the file.

58
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:38,340
 So the parameters to open are,

59
00:02:38,340 --> 00:02:40,280
 it takes one parameter that's required,

60
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:41,740
 which is the name of the file.

61
00:02:41,740 --> 00:02:42,920
 Another parameter that's optional,

62
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:44,880
 whether or not to read it or write it.

63
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:47,040
 If we're reading the file, it doesn't harm it.

64
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:48,080
 You can read it over and over.

65
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:50,160
 If you write it, it actually, if there's already data

66
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:52,160
 in that file, it truncates it and writes something.

67
00:02:52,160 --> 00:02:53,560
 And we're not gonna really write files,

68
00:02:53,560 --> 00:02:54,960
 we're mostly gonna read them.

69
00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:57,680
 And so open sort of, you pass it in a file,

70
00:02:57,680 --> 00:02:59,240
 it gives you back this file handle,

71
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:01,840
 and then you have a variable in which you store it.

72
00:03:01,840 --> 00:03:05,440
 I often call it fhand to be mnemonic.

73
00:03:05,440 --> 00:03:08,400
 Just, you'll see my code, I use fhand all the time

74
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:11,040
 to indicate that that is a file handle.

75
00:03:11,040 --> 00:03:15,760
 And so if we were to run this in an interactive mode,

76
00:03:15,760 --> 00:03:19,920
 we'll open mbox.txt, and that is a function

77
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:22,320
 built into Python, and then it gives us back a handle.

78
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:23,820
 It does not give the data.

79
00:03:23,820 --> 00:03:26,920
 You can kind of see this when we print out the file handle

80
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:28,440
 using the print statement.

81
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:31,240
 It doesn't print the lines that are in the file.

82
00:03:31,240 --> 00:03:32,880
 The lines that are in the file are sort of out there.

83
00:03:32,880 --> 00:03:36,760
 There could be like 10 million lines for all we know,

84
00:03:36,760 --> 00:03:37,760
 lines in the file.

85
00:03:37,760 --> 00:03:41,700
 The handle's like a little opening outside of your program.

86
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:43,880
 And you can talk to the file by opening it,

87
00:03:43,880 --> 00:03:45,480
 then you can read stuff.

88
00:03:45,480 --> 00:03:47,520
 If you're writing the file, you can write stuff.

89
00:03:47,520 --> 00:03:49,680
 And then you close the file to shut the handle down.

90
00:03:49,680 --> 00:03:53,440
 The handle is a thing that allows you to get to the file.

91
00:03:53,440 --> 00:03:56,480
 It is not the file itself, and it's not the data in the file.

92
00:03:56,480 --> 00:03:59,400
 It's just a wrapper that kind of allows you.

93
00:03:59,400 --> 00:04:00,680
 So this, if you print it out, it's like,

94
00:04:00,680 --> 00:04:02,720
 that's the file we opened, we're reading it.

95
00:04:02,720 --> 00:04:04,640
 And encoding has to do with the different kinds

96
00:04:04,640 --> 00:04:06,560
 of character sets, which we talked about

97
00:04:06,560 --> 00:04:07,600
 at the end of the last lecture,

98
00:04:07,600 --> 00:04:09,600
 the Unicode character set, et cetera.

99
00:04:09,600 --> 00:04:12,240
 UTF-8 is a great character set.

100
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:15,780
 It's probably the most typical character set

101
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:18,320
 that you will run into, although you can have

102
00:04:18,320 --> 00:04:19,720
 different character sets of files,

103
00:04:19,720 --> 00:04:21,220
 but most of them are UTF-8.

104
00:04:21,220 --> 00:04:25,180
 So, of course, this is Python.

105
00:04:25,180 --> 00:04:27,960
 If you make a mistake and there's a file that doesn't exist,

106
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:30,760
 we get a traceback, and it blows up.

107
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:36,560
 We'll show you in a second how to deal with that.

108
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:39,380
 Now, the newline character is an important part

109
00:04:39,380 --> 00:04:41,040
 of file reading.

110
00:04:41,040 --> 00:04:44,800
 And in files, in strings, we can put the newline character

111
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:47,700
 in by this \n character.

112
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:50,480
 And the \n is the character that indicates

113
00:04:50,480 --> 00:04:53,040
 that we're supposed to go to another line.

114
00:04:53,040 --> 00:04:54,680
 Go to a new line.

115
00:04:54,680 --> 00:04:55,920
 Go to a new line.

116
00:04:55,920 --> 00:04:57,120
 And so we have, what is this?

117
00:04:57,120 --> 00:04:59,460
 Well, that's a \n, that's a \n.

118
00:04:59,460 --> 00:05:04,120
 And so, if we print it out, we print it this way,

119
00:05:04,120 --> 00:05:05,900
 we see that the \n is in there.

120
00:05:05,900 --> 00:05:06,900
 This is how we type it.

121
00:05:06,900 --> 00:05:10,880
 We actually type \n to Python to indicate

122
00:05:10,880 --> 00:05:12,940
 that we're supposed to put that there.

123
00:05:12,940 --> 00:05:15,880
 But if we do a print statement,

124
00:05:15,880 --> 00:05:17,560
 it actually interprets the \n,

125
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:20,440
 and so the \n causes this movement to the beginning.

126
00:05:20,440 --> 00:05:22,800
 Now, the print actually, at the end of this,

127
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:24,120
 adds another \n.

128
00:05:24,120 --> 00:05:28,000
 So the \n that we put in by putting it into the string

129
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,720
 is that one, and then print always puts a \n at the end.

130
00:05:31,720 --> 00:05:36,040
 There's actually a way to override that \n behavior

131
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:37,840
 by putting something on the print statement,

132
00:05:37,840 --> 00:05:39,520
 which we'll talk about later.

133
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:44,360
 Now, it's important to note that the \n is one character.

134
00:05:44,360 --> 00:05:48,840
 And so, even though this x\ny prints this,

135
00:05:48,840 --> 00:05:51,400
 and then print adds another new line to go down to here,

136
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:52,840
 if you ask how many characters,

137
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:54,400
 what is the length of this?

138
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:55,800
 Well, it's only three.

139
00:05:55,800 --> 00:05:57,680
 That's because that's a character,

140
00:05:57,680 --> 00:06:00,200
 the \n's a character, and the y is a character.

141
00:06:00,200 --> 00:06:01,720
 So it's a three-character string.

142
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:05,320
 So the \n is a character like all the rest of the characters,

143
00:06:05,320 --> 00:06:10,020
 but it's only, we encode it by typing \n.

144
00:06:10,020 --> 00:06:12,560
 It's called an escape, where the \ is the escape,

145
00:06:12,560 --> 00:06:16,480
 \n is a way to say new line, 'cause we can't see it.

146
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:19,040
 It's a way for us to encode in a string

147
00:06:19,040 --> 00:06:22,640
 this non-printable character, this invisible character.

148
00:06:22,640 --> 00:06:24,760
 The white space, it's part of white space.

149
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:27,480
 So as we're reading through the file,

150
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:29,240
 we can think of it as a sequence of lines,

151
00:06:29,240 --> 00:06:31,140
 and we can read these a line at a time.

152
00:06:31,140 --> 00:06:33,640
 We can also read them a character at a time if we want.

153
00:06:33,640 --> 00:06:36,000
 And so, but it's more common to say, read this line,

154
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,160
 read the next line, read the line after that,

155
00:06:38,160 --> 00:06:39,960
 et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

156
00:06:39,960 --> 00:06:42,880
 But the way to best think about this,

157
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:44,080
 it doesn't really matter.

158
00:06:44,080 --> 00:06:45,500
 You can think about it as lines,

159
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:47,880
 and we will in most of the programs that we write.

160
00:06:47,880 --> 00:06:50,800
 But realize that the way when we see this,

161
00:06:50,800 --> 00:06:55,960
 we see it like this, it comes back to the beginning,

162
00:06:55,960 --> 00:06:56,800
 it comes back to the beginning.

163
00:06:56,800 --> 00:06:59,880
 There's a character in the file at each of these points

164
00:06:59,880 --> 00:07:01,120
 to say, go back to the beginning.

165
00:07:01,120 --> 00:07:04,360
 It's like hitting the Enter key on your computer.

166
00:07:04,360 --> 00:07:05,460
 And that is a new line.

167
00:07:05,460 --> 00:07:07,920
 So you have to think that in the file,

168
00:07:07,920 --> 00:07:11,240
 in order for your text editor and Python and everybody

169
00:07:11,240 --> 00:07:14,600
 to know where the lines end, you put new lines in the file.

170
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:16,360
 And that's another character.

171
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:19,680
 So, you know, this looks like an empty line.

172
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:21,340
 This line here looks like an empty line,

173
00:07:21,340 --> 00:07:22,680
 but really it has a single character,

174
00:07:22,680 --> 00:07:24,200
 and the character is a new line.

175
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:25,880
 And it turns out that in a bit,

176
00:07:25,880 --> 00:07:27,660
 we're gonna need to keep track of the fact

177
00:07:27,660 --> 00:07:31,360
 that every line is ended by a new line.

178
00:07:31,360 --> 00:07:33,120
 So up next, I'm gonna talk a little bit

179
00:07:33,120 --> 00:07:36,000
 about how to read files in Python.

180
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:38,580
 (gentle music)

